reveal.en:
The client of a Template Method should depend on the **abstract** base class, not on one concrete subclass:

```java
public class FulfillmentDesk {
    private final OrderWorkflow workflow;

    public FulfillmentDesk(OrderWorkflow workflow) {
        this.workflow = workflow;
    }

    public void handle(String orderId) {
        workflow.process(orderId);
    }
}
```

```mermaid
classDiagram
  class OrderWorkflow
  <<abstract>> OrderWorkflow
  class OnlineOrderWorkflow
  class StorePickupWorkflow
  class FulfillmentDesk
  OrderWorkflow <|-- OnlineOrderWorkflow
  OrderWorkflow <|-- StorePickupWorkflow
  FulfillmentDesk --> OrderWorkflow
```

With the abstract field type the desk can be handed a delivery workflow today and a pickup workflow tomorrow without a single edit, and it stays ignorant of how any of them validate or charge. With the concrete field type the desk is welded to one variant, and adding a variant means editing the desk too.

Notice the desk also calls only `process(orderId)` — one entry point. It never calls a step itself.

